  "content": {
    "id": "p16-q1-group",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "1",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p16-q1-instruction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Decide whether each expression gives a valid general definition of the derivative. Do not assume the derivative exists in advance.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p16-q1-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f'(x)=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False as a general definition. The symmetric limit can exist when the derivative does not.",
          "worked": "For $f(t)=|t|$ at $x=0$, the displayed limit is $0$, but $f'(0)$ does not exist. Where $f'(x)$ exists, the displayed limit does equal it.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q1-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f'(c)=\\lim_{x\\to c}\\dfrac{f(x)-f(c)}{x-c}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "This is the alternative derivative limit given in the page 11 student note.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q1-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$f'(x)=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\dfrac{f(x)-f(h)}{x-h}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. The two secant points do not both approach $x$.",
          "worked": "For $f(t)=t^2$ and $x=2$, the proposed limit is $\\dfrac{4-0}{2-0}=2$, while $f'(2)=4$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q1-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$f'(x)=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\dfrac{f(x+2h)-f(x)}{2h}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True. The increment is $2h$ in both the numerator and denominator.",
          "worked": "Let $k=2h$. As $h\\to0$, $k\\to0$, and the quotient becomes $\\lim_{k\\to0}\\dfrac{f(x+k)-f(x)}{k}=f'(x)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q1-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$f'(x)=\\lim_{\\Delta x\\to0}\\dfrac{f(x+\\Delta x)-f(x)}{\\Delta x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "$\\Delta x$ plays the same role as $h$ in the taught first-principles formula.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q1-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$f'(x)=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\dfrac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True. This is the backward-increment form.",
          "worked": "Let $k=-h$. Then $\\dfrac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h}=\\dfrac{f(x+k)-f(x)}{k}$, whose limit is $f'(x)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q1-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "$f'(x)=\\lim_{h\\to\\infty}h\\times\\dfrac{f\\left(x+\\frac{1}{h}\\right)-f\\left(x-\\frac{1}{h}\\right)}{2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False as a general definition. This symmetric limit can exist without $f'(x)$.",
          "worked": "Put $t=\\frac{1}{h}$, so $t\\to0^+$. The expression is the symmetric quotient $\\dfrac{f(x+t)-f(x-t)}{2t}$. For $f(u)=|u|$ at $x=0$, its limit is $0$ although $f'(0)$ does not exist. Where $f'(x)$ exists, the displayed limit does equal it.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
